/** Recordkeeping only. Nothing here says any of it is deductible. */
---

<Article
  slug="what-receipts-to-keep-trucking"
  toolLine="Four questions will tell you roughly how much of last year is currently undocumented."
>
  <p class="lede">
    The short answer is "all of them," which is useless advice. The useful
    version is knowing which ones you are most likely to lose, because those are
    the ones worth building a habit around.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is a recordkeeping post. It does not say what is deductible &mdash;
    that is a conversation for a tax professional. It says which paper tends to
    survive and which tends to vanish.
  </p>

  <h2>The ones you already keep</h2>

  <p>
    Some records look after themselves, and it is worth naming them so you can
    stop worrying about them:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Major repairs.</strong> Large, memorable, and accompanied by a proper printed invoice that usually goes straight into a folder.</li>
    <li><strong>Tires.</strong> Same story &mdash; infrequent, expensive, properly documented.</li>
    <li><strong>Permits and licences.</strong> Official paperwork, treated as important because it obviously is.</li>
    <li><strong>Anything financed.</strong> There is a contract and a counterparty who also holds a copy.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These are not where the problem lives. Notice the pattern: big amount,
    infrequent, proper invoice, obviously significant. Every attribute that
    makes a record survive.
  </p>

  <h2>The ones that disappear</h2>

  <p>
    Now invert every one of those attributes and you have the list of what
    actually goes missing:
  </p>

  <h3>Fuel</h3>
  <p>
    The largest total of the year, made up of individually unremarkable
    transactions, printed on thermal paper, handed to you outdoors while you are
    in a hurry. Every risk factor at once. The single most important record to
    capture immediately.
  </p>

  <h3>Tolls</h3>
  <p>
    Small enough individually to feel not worth the effort. Where a transponder
    account produces a statement, you have a durable record. Where you paid
    cash, the slip is the only evidence that will ever exist.
  </p>

  <h3>Scale tickets</h3>
  <p>
    Thermal, small, and handled at exactly the moment you have least attention
    to spare. Covered in more detail in
    <a href="/blog/scale-tickets-and-lumper-fees">scale tickets and lumper fees</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Lumper fees</h3>
  <p>
    Frequently cash, sometimes with no receipt offered at all unless you ask.
    The hardest record in the whole job to hold on to.
  </p>

  <h3>Showers and parking</h3>
  <p>
    Individually trivial, which is precisely why nobody keeps them, and
    collectively meaningful over fifty weeks. The arithmetic is the argument: a
    small amount several times a week for a year is not small.
  </p>

  <h3>Truck stop supplies</h3>
  <p>
    Gloves, straps, cleaning supplies, a replacement cable, a bulb. Bought
    alongside other things, often on the same transaction as fuel and a meal
    &mdash; which is why the card statement is not a substitute for the receipt.
    The statement shows one total to one merchant and cannot separate the three.
  </p>

  <h3>Roadside and emergency spending</h3>
  <p>
    Bought under pressure, at odd hours, when documentation is the last thing on
    your mind. Also frequently the largest unplanned amounts of the year.
  </p>

  <h2>The pattern worth internalising</h2>

  <p>
    Every record that goes missing shares the same profile: small enough to feel
    unimportant, frequent enough to add up, printed on paper that degrades, and
    received at a moment when you are busy.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is not a memory problem or a discipline problem. It is a design problem
    &mdash; the moment a receipt is generated is systematically the worst
    possible moment to ask someone to file it carefully.
  </p>

  <p>
    Which is why "be more organised" fails as advice, and why the only thing
    that works is making capture fast enough to happen at the counter. Three
    seconds is a habit. Anything longer competes with the job.
  </p>

  <h2>What about the digital ones</h2>

  <p>
    Increasingly some of this arrives as email or in an app &mdash; toll
    accounts, app-based parking, invoices from shops that have gone paperless.
    Those are more durable than thermal paper, but they introduce the other
    failure mode: fragmentation.
  </p>

  <p>
    A record you cannot find is not meaningfully different from a record you do
    not have. If the fuel receipts are in one place and the toll statements are
    in an email account and the parking is in three different apps, then at the
    end of the year you cannot tell what is missing, because you have no single
    place where its absence would be visible.
  </p>

  <p>
    Getting everything into one destination matters roughly as much as capturing
    it in the first place.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>

  <p>
    Capture everything, but if you are building the habit one step at a time,
    start with fuel &mdash; biggest total, most frequent, worst paper. Then the
    small stuff nobody keeps, because that is where the quiet losses are. The
    big invoices will keep looking after themselves.
  </p>

  <p>
    For how long any of it needs to survive afterwards, see
    <a href="/guide/how-long-to-keep-tax-records">how long to keep tax records</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
